@meta
id: 02-13
title: Undo by the clock, not the count
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: undo, redo, undo-branches
par: 91
minutes: 6
vscode: nothing — there is no equivalent
open: subscription.md

@brief
Counting changes works when there are three of them. After twenty minutes
of editing you have no idea how many there were, but you do know two other
things: roughly how long ago the file was good, and when you last wrote
it. Vim can undo by both.

`:earlier 10s` rewinds ten seconds of editing. `:earlier 5m` rewinds five
minutes. `:later` goes the other way. And the useful one: `:earlier 1f`
rewinds to how the file looked at the previous *write*, however many
changes ago that was.

The subscription rules are being edited live during a call. In order:

  1. Append ` (calendar days)` to the Trial line. Write the file.

  2. Append ` then cancels` to the Renewal line.

  3. Append ` and day 14` to the Dunning line.

  4. The call ends with "ignore everything after we saved". Go back to the
     state the file was in at your write, in one command, without counting
     anything.

  5. Then someone adds: keep the renewal note, it was right. Step forward
     one change.

Save. Trial and Renewal carry their additions, Dunning does not.

@keys
| `:earlier 1f` | back to the state at the previous file write |
| `:earlier 30s` | back thirty seconds of editing |
| `:later 1` | forward one change |
| `:later 1f` | forward to the next write |
| `g-` `g+` | back and forward one state, without the command line |

@hints
Step 4 is one command and the `f` in it stands for file write, not for
anything to do with counts.
--
Step 5 goes forward again. The opposite of `:earlier` is `:later`, and
with a plain number it counts changes.
--
`:earlier 1f<Enter>` then `:later 1<Enter>`, then write and quit. After
the rewind vim considers the buffer unmodified, because it matches what is
on disk — that is how you know the rewind landed where you asked.

@solution
    jj $ a  (calendar days) <Esc> :w<Enter>
    j $ a  then cancels <Esc>
    j $ a  and day 14 <Esc>
    :earlier 1f<Enter>
    :later 1<Enter>
    ZZ

`:earlier 1f` is the command to remember from this chapter. It is `:q!`
without quitting and without losing the chance to change your mind, and it
turns every `:w` you type into a save point you can return to. Write
often, and this gets more useful the more you do it.

The time based forms are for the other situation — where you cannot say
how many changes ago it was, only that it was before lunch. `:earlier 1h`
is a real thing to type, and vim will get it right.

Two warnings. All of this lives in memory, so it dies when you quit. You
can change that with two lines in a vimrc:

    set undofile
    set undodir=~/.vim/undo

With those, vim writes the whole undo history to a file beside your undo
directory when you save, and reloads it next time you open the file. Undo
across days, across reboots. Chapter 28 builds the vimrc; know now that it
is available, because the default is to lose everything on quit.

And `:earlier` counts editing time, not wall clock time. Ten minutes at
the editor with the last three spent reading is not `:earlier 10m`.

@start subscription.md
# Subscription billing

- Trial is 14 days
- Renewal retries twice
- Dunning emails on day 3 and day 7

@expected subscription.md
# Subscription billing

- Trial is 14 days (calendar days)
- Renewal retries twice then cancels
- Dunning emails on day 3 and day 7
